{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790714733,"nonce":"8iV+fh2QQLu9BpQ/tYxpVk5tRpo=","message":"Python constructor follow-up, 2026-09-29.\n\nTwo common-path costs remained after ownership was corrected:\n\n- every successful foreign constructor called $Foreign$error_kind, reading thread-local state and entering string/error support even though a constructor's successful result must be a non-null object;\n- Caribou represented a returned core object as a Zyntax DynamicBox pointing to a separately allocated Held::Object, so each construction allocated and freed both the language box and the adapter cell.\n\nZyntax 685d64cc makes null the constructor error sentinel and queries the stored error only on failure. Caribou 0d86e32 encodes the compact Caribou Handle directly in the foreign box's opaque word, validates constructor results as objects, and bypasses generic scalar/string/tuple result classification. Metadata values remain aligned Held pointers, distinguished by the word's low bit.\n\nThe machine was heavily contended, so profiler sampled-thread CPU time is the stable before/after measure. Each profile covers two warm and one measured run of 20 million constructions:\n\n| path | CPU before | CPU after | reduction |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Python→Haxe | 12.486 s | 6.790 s | 45.6% |\n| Python→Wren | 15.120 s | 11.194 s | 26.0% |\n\nThe paired post-change wall measurements were 189.3 ns Python→Haxe and 195.6 ns Python→Wren. The Wren-specific discrepancy is therefore about 6 ns in that run; most remaining time is the shared Python foreign-object path plus the target runtime's allocation.\n\nPython→Python's approximately 5 ns table entry is not an allocation baseline: Zyntax scalar-replaces the loop's local Python object because only its final float field is observed. Cross-language objects necessarily escape into another managed heap and cannot receive that optimization.\n\nProfiles no longer contain foreign_error_kind, zrtl_string_equals, generic Str/Int64 result tests, the second malloc/free pair, or Held::Object drop glue. Remaining common costs include the one required DynamicBox allocation, Caribou handle creation/release, Zyntax foreign dispatch, and the Python loop. Eliminating the box and handle would require an unboxed HostObject representation whose ownership and root are explicit in Zyntax IR.\n\nValidation: zyntax_python foreign integration passes; Caribou release python_foreign and python_haxe integration tests pass. The two 60-million-call profile runs also exercise replacement and release without unbounded retention.","files":null}]}